Well, I didn't write down the name of the person recommended earlier to
send ideas for changing the user interface to, so I'll post these. If
somebody wants to shoot me the address of the dev team, I'd be happy to
forward this.
This comes from considerable experience with Moria and Hack, and some
recent experience with Nethack. Basically, I agree that Nethack's
weakest link is its interface, which is clunky and awkward and almost
cancels out its consiedrable strengths as a game. It seems easily
fixable with a few rather simple changes:

1) Make the "go until something interesting is found" command, now
<shift>G dir, be a single non-shofted keystroke plus the direction.
Might seem trivial, but when you think of how times you use that
command, it adds up to a lot of keystrokes saved.
2) The player shouldn't have to remove something first in order to
replace it with something similar. For instance, if you're wearing ring
mail and want to put on your splint mail, you should be able to just put
on the splint mail and have the game assume you meant to remove the ring
mail first, and do it for you (assuming, of course, that it's not
cursed).
3) Is there a reason for the right hand/left hand question when putting
on a ring? If not, it ought to be removed. All unnecessary prompts and
"-more-" lines should be removed.
4) Is there a reason for having two different commands for donning armor
and rings/amulets? If not, combine them into a single "wear" command.
5) There should be a way to get an inventory list of all the items in a
pile you`re standing on.
6) When you try to wield, wear, put on, take off, remove, eat, apply,
read, quaff, zap, Zap, throw something, the list of the appropriate
items in your pack should be automatically displayed (like if you done
I-whatever). This could be an option which could be turned off by people
annoyed by it, but the default should be ON.
7) The / command, to look at something, should be cleaned up. It has too
many keystrokes involved in it currently (too many -more- things).

I'm not a programmer, but surely these wouldn't be hard to implement. I
think they would make the game surprisingly more playable and enjoyable.

